<!--
  Swipey's mark — the Flick — as the launcher icon's foreground.

  One card already leaving, and the two strokes it left behind. It is the verb rather than
  the noun: the app is not a pile of photographs, it is the act of getting through one. The
  card leans 13°, which is near enough the tilt the deck itself gives a card under a
  dragging thumb.

  ## Geometry
  The rotation is baked into the coordinates rather than expressed as a <group> transform,
  and that is deliberate. These same paths appear three times — here, in
  ic_launcher_monochrome.xml, and as a Compose ImageVector in ui/design/Mark.kt — and a
  transform that one of the three applied differently would be a drift nothing could catch.
  Flat coordinates compare as values, which is what MarkGeometryTest does with them.

  Every arc is a minor arc with the sweep flag set, and MarkGeometryTest relies on that to
  locate each corner's centre and prove the ink stays inside the 33dp circle every launcher
  mask is guaranteed to show. The card's furthest ink reaches 27.4dp of that 33dp budget.

  That margin is the artwork's size, not a safety factor left over from drawing it. The first
  cut of this mark sat at 32.25dp — inside the circle, and still wrong: it crowded every mask
  it survived, and on a squircle the card's corner ran almost into the tile's own. A launcher
  icon is read at 48dp among forty others, and the space around a mark is what separates it
  from its neighbours. Uniformly scaled to 85% about the canvas centre, radii included, so it
  is the same shape and not a redrawn one.

  ## Colours
  Literals, not @color references, and deliberately so: a launcher icon must not follow the
  device's light/dark setting, and a values-night override would make it do exactly that.

  The card is the app's textPrimary and the trails its textDisabled, on the canvas — the
  "paper on ink" scheme. There is no accent here at all: Swipey spends its one blue on
  *keep*, inside the app, and an icon that also used it would be spending it on nothing.
  The trails sit at about 2.5:1 on the ground, which is far too little for text and right
  for these — they are a wake, and a wake that competed with the card would be a second
  subject.
-->
